# SRG-APP-000503-AU-000280  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must generate audit records when
# successful/unsuccessful logon attempts occur.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000503-AU-000280
group:           V-221928
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000172
control_rev5:    AU-12

contribution:    Splunk writes its own authentication successes and failures to its audit
                 trail; the application's configurable share is whether those events are
                 routed to the index so that they become reviewable audit records rather
                 than file-only output.

governs:         audit.conf - The audit trail Splunk keeps of its own activity, and how those records
                 are signed.

check:           audit.conf [auditTrail] queueing in 1|true

external:        Logon attempts resolved by an external directory service or SSO provider
                 are recorded by that provider; Splunk generates records only for the
                 attempts it processes itself, so the full logon record set depends on the
                 identity provider's own auditing.
external:        Retention and protection of the audit index files rest on the operating
                 system and the storage platform.

citation:        audit.conf.spec:23 [auditTrail], :24 queueing = <boolean>, :27-29 "If set
                 to false, you must add an inputs.conf stanza to tail the audit log", :29
                 "Default: true"; inputs.conf.spec:235 [monitor://<path>], :1495 index,
                 :1501 signedaudit = <boolean>. No condition is written over audit.conf
                 [auditTrail] logging_format: that key is not declared in the staged
                 specification set, so its vocabulary cannot be confirmed.
finding:         Assessed at the application layer alone: audit event queueing is disabled
                 and no monitor input tails the audit log into an index, so the logon
                 records Splunk generates never become searchable audit records.
